<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hera</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Juno (Roman)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hera was the queen of the Greek gods, wife and sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and goddess of marriage and women. Her name appears on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets from Pylos, and her great sanctuaries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> were among the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>; colonists built major temples to her at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paestum">Paestum</a> (Poseidonia) and Metapontum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified her with Juno.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hera.jpg" alt="Hera" width="320" height="584" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hera_Campana_Louvre_Ma2283.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
